WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.407
Now let us focus on INNER JOIN.

00:00:02.407 --> 00:00:05.847
Look first at the small tables at the top of the slide.

00:00:05.847 --> 00:00:13.427
Ana has two matching admissions, Ben has no matching admission, and Cara has one matching admission.

00:00:13.427 --> 00:00:20.157
Admission 104 belongs to patient ID 4, which does not exist in the small patient table.

00:00:20.157 --> 00:00:24.082
The INNER JOIN result contains only matching combinations.

00:00:24.082 --> 00:00:27.627
Ana appears twice because she has two admissions.

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:29.312
Cara appears once.

00:00:29.312 --> 00:00:35.352
Ben disappears because there is no matching admission, and admission 104 disappears because there is no

00:00:35.352 --> 00:00:40.352
matching patient. This is the main idea of INNER JOIN: both sides must participate in a match.

00:00:40.352 --> 00:00:43.337
Now look at the runnable PostgreSQL query.

00:00:43.337 --> 00:00:48.542
We give patients the alias p and admissions the alias a.

00:00:48.542 --> 00:00:54.202
The ON condition says that an admission matches a patient when their patient IDs are equal.

00:00:54.202 --> 00:00:56.952
The selected columns combine information from both tables.

00:00:56.952 --> 00:01:03.657
In the current Module 6 practice database, there are twelve admission records,

00:01:03.657 --> 00:01:05.607
and every admission references a valid patient.

00:01:05.607 --> 00:01:09.672
Therefore this INNER JOIN returns exactly twelve rows.

00:01:09.672 --> 00:01:13.322
That does not mean there are twelve different patients.

00:01:13.322 --> 00:01:16.152
Some patients have more than one admission.

00:01:16.152 --> 00:01:23.017
The grain is one matching admission with patient information attached.

00:01:23.017 --> 00:01:27.057
When you run the query, do not only ask whether it executes.

00:01:27.057 --> 00:01:29.597
Compare the result with your prediction.

00:01:29.597 --> 00:01:35.769
Check repeated patient IDs and confirm that each repeated row represents a different admission.
